ptVersion":1},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"America","confidence":0.86}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"The erosion of the corporate social contract has caused major losses in job security, rising pay, pensions, health benefits, and other supports for American workers.","confidence":0.94},"academicOrientation":{"score":34,"confidence":0.92},"profileConfidence":0.94,"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"3ea537fc1da99ec1f165f10c68e7b5d3b2a1434d28ff38013cfac992f8692702","reviewStatus":"unreviewed","validationWarnings":[]},"primarySubject":"Business & Economics","secondarySubjects":["American History","Society & Culture"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily explores the evolution of labor relations and corporate responsibility in the United States, framing its analysis within the context of American economic history and societal impacts.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"05df060b69b1871a02e2fcc6714c06d21ea5ae2ffcfcaf1d6f2778b521652233","summary":"In this richly detailed and eye-opening book, Rick Wartzman chronicles the erosion of the relationship between American companies and their workers. Through the stories of four major employers--General Motors, General Electric, Kodak, and Coca-Cola--he shows how big businesses once took responsibility for providing their workers and retirees with an array of social benefits. At the height of the post-World War II economy, these companies also believed that worker pay needed to be kept high in order to preserve morale and keep the economy humming. Productivity boomed. But the corporate social contract didn't last. By tracing the ups and downs of these four corporate icons over seventy years, Wartzman illustrates just how much has been lost: job security and steadily rising pay, guaranteed pensions, robust health benefits, and much more. Charting the Golden Age of the '50s and '60s; the turbulent years of the '70s and '80s; and the growth of downsizing, outsourcing, and instability in the modern era, Wartzman's narrative is a biography of the American Dream gone sideways. Deeply researched and compelling, The End of Loyalty will make you rethink how Americans can begin to resurrect the middle class.","publicationYear":2017,"displaySummary":"In this richly detailed and eye-opening book, Rick Wartzman chronicles the erosion of the relationship between American companies and their workers. Through the stories of four major employers--General Motors, General Electric, Kodak, and Coca-Cola--he shows how big businesses once took responsibility for providing their workers and retirees with an array of social benefits. At the height of the post-World War II economy, these companies also believed that worker pay needed to be kept high in order to preserve morale and keep the economy humming. Productivity boomed. But the corporate social contract didn't last. By tracing the ups and downs of these four corporate icons over seventy years, Wartzman illustrates just how much has been lost: job security and steadily rising pay, guaranteed pensions, robust health benefits, and much more. Charting the Golden Age of the '50s and '60s; the turbulent years of the '70s and '80s; and the growth of downsizing, outsourcing, and instability in the modern era, Wartzman's narrative is a biography of the American Dream gone sideways. Deeply researched and compelling, The End of Loyalty will make you rethink how Americans can begin to resurrect the middle class.","primaryTopic":"Business, Capitalism & Corporations","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Business & Economics","confidence":"high","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Business & Economics","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Business & Economics","mappedSubject":"Business & Economics","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book primarily explores the evolution of lab